<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/glossary#earl-of-mercia">Glossary</a> · Term · title</p><h1 class="enc-title">Earl of Mercia</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">11th century</span> · <a href="/centuries/11th-century">11th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Earl of Mercia was one of the great territorial earls of late <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anglo-saxons">Anglo-Saxon</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>, governing much of the old Mercian kingdom under the kings of England. Under Cnut and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edward-the-confessor">Edward the Confessor</a> the earldom was held by Leofric and then by his son Ælfgar and grandson Edwin. Edwin submitted to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-the-conqueror">William the Conqueror</a> after 1066 but rebelled and was killed in 1071, after which the earldom lapsed.</p></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The name survived the kingdom in the great <b>Earldom of Mercia</b> of the 11th century, held by Leofric and his family, and Offa's Dyke remains one of the best-known monuments of early medieval Britain.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-mercia">Kingdom of Mercia · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li></ul></section>
